  "text": "Here's a Python function that meets the requirements:\n\n### \u2705 Function Description:\n- **Input:** A string\n- **Output:** A dictionary where:\n  - Keys are **lowercased words** from the string\n  - Values are the **frequency** of each word\n- **Ignore case** and **punctuation**\n\n---\n\n### \u2705 Python Function:\n\n```python\nimport string\n\ndef word_histogram(text):\n    # Remove punctuation and convert to lowercase\n    translator = str.maketrans('', '', string.punctuation)\n    cleaned = text.translate(translator).lower()\n\n    # Split into words\n    words = cleaned.split()\n\n    # Count frequency\n    histogram = {}\n    for word in words:\n        histogram[word] = histogram.get(word, 0) + 1\n\n    return histogram\n```\n\n---\n\n### \u2705 Example Usage:\n\n```python\nprint(word_histogram(\"Hello, world! Hello.\"))\n# Output: {'hello': 2, 'world': 1}\n\nprint(word_histogram(\"Python is great. Python is fun!\"))\n# Output: {'python': 2, 'is': 2, 'great': 1, 'fun': 1}\n\nprint(word_histogram(\"Test, test, TEST.\"))\n# Output: {'test': 3}\n```\n\n---\n\n### \ud83e\uddea Unit Tests (as per your request):\n\n```python\nassert word_histogram(\"Hello, world! Hello.\") == {\"hello\": 2, \"world\": 1}\nassert word_histogram(\"Python is great. Python is fun!\") == {\"python\": 2, \"is\": 2, \"great\": 1, \"fun\": 1}\nassert word_histogram(\"Test, test, TEST.\") == {\"test\": 3}\n```\n\nLet me know if you need help with this in another language or want a version that uses `collections.Counter`!",
